\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why the agentic interface is a consensus abstraction}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{agent}
\tag{reinforcement-learning}

\p{The objects in \ref{ftip-0009} come from the common core of Markov and
partially observed control, autoregressive generation, and the modern agent
literature. \citef{murphy2024reinforcement} motivates the stochastic-control
semantics, while \citef{wang2024agents} illustrates software-agent interfaces
in which observations, tools, and evaluators matter as much as emitted text.
Language-model papers often flatten these objects into a prompt and response;
FTIP keeps them distinct so sandbox and long-horizon costs remain visible.}

\p{The distinction between hidden state and public history enforces
non-anticipation: a policy may depend only on information causally available
when it acts. It also prevents a verifier's privileged state or a future test
result from leaking into the rollout policy. Conversely, a training protocol
may use a terminal outcome after the trajectory has finished, provided that
the resulting feedback is typed as post hoc training information.}

\p{The first theorem questions concern equivalence and compounding. Under what
conditions does a token-level policy induce the same law as an utterance-level
policy? If every required transition has conditional validity at most #{p_t},
how does end-to-end success scale with horizon? Which coverage condition lets a
training task distribution control independent environments? These are the
bridges from elementary probability to agentic scaling and limitation results.}
